{"title":"making a zine for the UN","link":"https://keningzhu.com/journal/making-a-zine-for-the-un","date":1759734360000,"content":" \n\n\n  <p>recently, I worked with a group of colleagues within the United Nations to transform a paper they wrote — called <em>“Survival or Renewal: Imagining the UN’s Next Horizon”</em> — into an illustrated zine. they published the paper under the name “<a href=\"https://www.departmentofthepossible.org/\" target=\"_blank\">Department of the Possible</a>,” and shared it online during the weeks of the UN General Assembly. </p><p>I’ve made a few zines in the last years: <a href=\"https://keningzhu.com/journal/creative-systems-zine\" target=\"_blank\">on deconstructing the productivity machine</a>, on exploring <a href=\"https://keningzhu.com/journal/sleep-zine\" target=\"_blank\">the ritual of sleep</a>, amongst many spontaneous <a href=\"https://keningzhu.com/journal/microbooks-for-interdimensional-traveling\" target=\"_blank\">paper things</a>. this was the first illustrated zine I did for a client — and in particular, an institutionally-affliated client. </p><p>the whole thing took me around ~2 weeks — but not in a totally linear way. many days, it was all I could think about. on other days, I lived my life, traveled to Izmir, visited friends, wrote my newsletter, edited my podcast episode, worked on launching <a href=\"https://keningzhu.com/labyrinth\">labyrinth</a>, and then, spent many late nights working on the zine until 2 or 3 AM, after all my friends had long gone to sleep. </p>\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n  \n  \n\n\n\n\n\n \n  <a href=\"https://drive.google.com/file/d/1oZPUuwBT5Vb23D1B4IJ-K6g0oYTVzbl4/view\" target=\"_blank\">\n    download the zine here\n  </a>\n  \n  \n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n  \n\n    \n  \n    \n\n      \n\n      \n        <figure>\n          \n        \n        \n\n        \n          \n            \n          \n            \n                \n                \n                \n                \n                \n                \n                \n                <img src=\"https://images.squarespace-cdn.com/content/v1/51e8148de4b01c1eb79c1977/442f86b0-13b3-48c7-8884-4661fa108701/zoom+out+figma.png?format=1000w\" width=\"2262\" height=\"2096\" srcset=\"https://images.squarespace-cdn.com/content/v1/51e8148de4b01c1eb79c1977/442f86b0-13b3-48c7-8884-4661fa108701/zoom+out+figma.png?format=100w 100w, https://images.squarespace-cdn.com/content/v1/51e8148de4b01c1eb79c1977/442f86b0-13b3-48c7-8884-4661fa108701/zoom+out+figma.png?format=300w 300w, https://images.squarespace-cdn.com/content/v1/51e8148de4b01c1eb79c1977/442f86b0-13b3-48c7-8884-4661fa108701/zoom+out+figma.png?format=500w 500w, https://images.squarespace-cdn.com/content/v1/51e8148de4b01c1eb79c1977/442f86b0-13b3-48c7-8884-4661fa108701/zoom+out+figma.png?format=750w 750w, https://images.squarespace-cdn.com/content/v1/51e8148de4b01c1eb79c1977/442f86b0-13b3-48c7-8884-4661fa108701/zoom+out+figma.png?format=1000w 1000w, https://images.squarespace-cdn.com/content/v1/51e8148de4b01c1eb79c1977/442f86b0-13b3-48c7-8884-4661fa108701/zoom+out+figma.png?format=1500w 1500w, https://images.squarespace-cdn.com/content/v1/51e8148de4b01c1eb79c1977/442f86b0-13b3-48c7-8884-4661fa108701/zoom+out+figma.png?format=2500w 2500w\" loading=\"lazy\" />\n\n            \n          \n        \n          \n        \n\n        \n          \n          <figcaption>\n            <p>near finished draft </p>\n          </figcaption>\n        \n      \n        </figure>\n      \n\n    \n  \n\n\n  \n\n\n \n\n\n  <h1>visiblity, institution &amp; artist  </h1><p>on one hand, this felt like a “big” project — in that the scope of vision (policy and systems transformation) was <em>so</em> big, the subject itself (the UN as an 80 year old leviathan institution) was big, and the potential reach (members and leaders within the UN) felt intimidatingly big. </p><p>in that respect, it challenged my feelings of visibility and reach. I had been very comfortable making work in my dark, soft, lunar cave corner of the internet, sending work <a href=\"https://keningzhu.com/journal/creative-courage-in-the-digital-void\" target=\"_blank\">into the void</a>, attracting a niche audience of mostly post-platform, independent artists and thinkers, and now — I was being asked to make work that would be shared in the light of the sun. or, potentially, a fluorescent-lit room in a <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Headquarters_of_the_United_Nations\" target=\"_blank\">skyscraper complex</a>? </p><p>but the colleagues that hired me — they <em>wanted</em> something different. I was invited into this project by a student of my <a href=\"https://keningzhu.com/creative-systems\">creative systems course</a> (hi Ruth!) who knew that the way I translated dense ideas into visual expressions in my courses. it took me a few iterations and exchanges with Ruth to realize that they didn’t want me to be a “traditional designer.” they didn’t want what I unconsciously projected as “UN-appropriate work.” they wanted to work with me <em>specifically for </em>my style and approach, for my color sensibilities, my dance of free-flowing spontaneity, and metaphorical density. <br /></p><h1>alignment &amp; purpose </h1><p>in other words, this was not a client that wanted something different than what I deeply, truly wanted to do. <strong>I was not being asked to compromise. I was asked to be <em>more</em> myself — for them.</strong> my life’s work <em>is</em> to use art as a vessel to carry visionary, transformative purpose. this project was exactly that. once I realized that, I felt a creative freedom — that, I could say, I’ve rarely felt with client projects. for that, I was deeply grateful. </p><p>as an independent artist of the internet wild — I’ve spent my career deliberately constructing a life on the edges, distancing myself from institutions — (probably because I grew up in an immigrant culture that trained me to chase them for validation, approval, and security). </p><p>the idea of me designing a zine for the United Nations — even if unofficially affliated, organized by a group of colleagues — it had an elegant feeling of integration to it, of coming full circle in my creative work and power. </p>\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n  \n  \n\n\n\n \n\n\n  <h1>on process </h1><p>I think, as a result, that’s probably why this project felt effortless — even while being extremely time and energy intensive, even while I went beyond in scope. it’s hard for me to be anything but all-or-nothing in creative projects. this negotiation of energy expenditure x energy recieved x desire/compulsion/commitment — it is always a dance, in particular with client work — and the equation is complex. </p><p>I had been stepping away from client work for this reason, but this particular project was definitely a net positive, probably because it was so contained and focused. my clients were wonderful to work with, I felt deeply aligned doing the work, and the process, while intense, was completed in two weeks. it felt like a true collaborative co-creation and emergence, which felt fulfilling. <br /></p><p><strong>SUMMARY OF HOW I MADE THIS (~2 weeks), with collaborative feedback at each round </strong></p><ol><li><p>I read the paper text a few times </p></li><li><p>I sketched a few concepts for each of the 4 primary illustrations. </p></li><li><p>I sketched initial cover concepts </p></li><li><p>I refined the illustrations for each concept </p></li><li><p>I made a quick 0.1 version of the design layout </p></li><li><p>I made a re-imagined version 0.2 of just one section — with new layout, additional graphics, &amp; minimalist illustrations </p></li><li><p>I made each major illustration </p></li><li><p>I created all the other pages, including aforementioned graphic sets </p></li><li><p>I made drafts of the cover </p></li><li><p>I did many rounds of refinement on the cover</p></li><li><p>I did many rounds of refinement on the entire paper </p></li><li><p>I compressed all the visual files and re-uploaded them </p></li><li><p>I exported and packaged all the assets</p></li></ol><p></p><h1>zine as visual world </h1><p>through the process, I did get the clear sense that making an illustrated zine was not unlike making a visual world of any sort — not unlike my philosophy to building <a href=\"https://keningzhu.com/worldbuilding\" target=\"_blank\">websites as worlds</a> — in that a zine had its own visual language, textures, main characters, internal logic, and structure. I had usually taken a more minimalist approach in making my own personal zines, but a project like this helped me see that this visual approach could be systemized to create and propogate many more zine-worlds. <br /><br /><strong>here’s images of work in progress:</strong> </p><h1>primary illustration concepts </h1>\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n  \n  \n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n  \n\n    \n  \n    \n\n      \n\n      \n        <figure>\n          \n        \n        \n\n        \n          \n            \n          \n            \n                \n                \n                \n                \n                \n                \n                \n                <img src=\"https://images.squarespace-cdn.com/content/v1/51e8148de4b01c1eb79c1977/dc76935e-df7c-4b21-b601-e475a9d45308/cover+sketch.png?format=1000w\" width=\"4988\" height=\"5596\" srcset=\"https://images.squarespace-cdn.com/content/v1/51e8148de4b01c1eb79c1977/dc76935e-df7c-4b21-b601-e475a9d45308/cover+sketch.png?format=100w 100w, https://images.squarespace-cdn.com/content/v1/51e8148de4b01c1eb79c1977/dc76935e-df7c-4b21-b601-e475a9d45308/cover+sketch.png?format=300w 300w, https://images.squarespace-cdn.com/content/v1/51e8148de4b01c1eb79c1977/dc76935e-df7c-4b21-b601-e475a9d45308/cover+sketch.png?format=500w 